  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Yellow porecrust (Amyloporia xantha) is a wood-decaying fungus in the Adustoporiaceae family, characterized by its bright yellow to golden coloration and tiny pores on its underside. This polypore fungus plays an important ecological role as a decomposer of dead wood in forest ecosystems.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Bright Yellow Color",
      "description": "Distinctive golden-yellow to bright yellow fruiting body that is easily visible on wood",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Tiny Pores",
      "description": "The underside features very small, rounded pores (2-3 per millimeter) rather than gills",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Thin Crust-like Form",
      "description": "Forms a thin, crusty or shelf-like growth tightly adhered to the wood substrate",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Yellow porecrust is commonly found on dead hardwood logs, branches, and stumps in mixed deciduous and coniferous forests throughout New England.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "The vibrant yellow pigmentation in Amyloporia xantha may help the fungus compete with other wood decomposers by making it easily recognizable and potentially deterring certain organisms from the same resource.",
